1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,173
What if your salary doubled, and ten years later you were exactly as broke as before?

2
00:00:05,493 --> 00:00:11,240
Same stress at the end of the month. Same empty savings account. Just a nicer car parked outside.

3
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,797
It happens to millions of people, and it has a name. Lifestyle inflation.

4
00:00:16,117 --> 00:00:24,836
Today, two versions of you get the exact same raises. One of them ends up with almost four hundred thousand dollars. The other one ends up with a very nice sofa.

5
00:00:25,636 --> 00:00:31,929
It's twenty twelve. You're twenty five, with your first real job. Forty thousand dollars a year, in this example.

6
00:00:32,249 --> 00:00:38,681
You share a small apartment with Max. Your sofa came from the street. Dinner is noodles, and you're honestly kind of happy.

7
00:00:39,001 --> 00:00:44,957
Every year, you get a raise of four thousand dollars. By twenty twenty two, you're making eighty thousand. Double.

8
00:00:45,277 --> 00:00:48,156
And here's where your life splits into two timelines.

9
00:00:48,956 --> 00:00:52,590
Timeline A. First raise. Four thousand dollars more a year.

10
00:00:52,910 --> 00:01:00,503
That's about three hundred and thirty a month, before tax. You deserve something. You move out from Max's place into your own apartment.

11
00:01:00,823 --> 00:01:05,444
The new rent eats most of the raise. But hey, no more roommate stealing your yoghurt.

12
00:01:05,764 --> 00:01:14,378
Next year, another raise. Time for a car payment. The year after that, a better gym, a few subscriptions, and dinners out instead of noodles.

13
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:19,996
Each upgrade feels small and reasonable on its own. That's what makes it dangerous.

14
00:01:20,316 --> 00:01:28,223
A bigger apartment. A car with heated seats. Takeaway three times a week because you're tired. Holidays that need another holiday to recover from.

15
00:01:28,543 --> 00:01:35,311
None of these are bad things. The problem is that every single raise gets eaten before it ever reaches a savings account.

16
00:01:35,631 --> 00:01:44,304
By twenty twenty two, timeline A earns twice as much. And at the end of every month, the bank balance looks exactly like it did when you ate noodles.

17
00:01:45,104 --> 00:01:50,294
Quick reality check about raises. A four thousand dollar raise is not four thousand dollars in your pocket.

18
00:01:50,614 --> 00:01:54,004
After income tax and other deductions, depending on where you live, a good chunk of it never reaches your account.

19
00:01:54,324 --> 00:02:05,353
That's why a raise often feels smaller than it sounded in the meeting. And why it disappears so easily into a slightly nicer life.

20
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:11,664
Timeline B's half and half split is easier when it's set up before the first bigger paycheck ever arrives.

21
00:02:12,464 --> 00:02:14,995
Timeline B gets the exact same raises. But makes one simple rule on day one.

22
00:02:15,315 --> 00:02:24,731
Half of every raise is for life. Half is for the future.

23
00:02:25,051 --> 00:02:32,179
So with the first four thousand dollar raise, two thousand goes to life upgrades. And two thousand a year goes automatically into an index fund. About one hundred and sixty seven dollars a month.

24
00:02:32,499 --> 00:02:38,502
Next raise, the same split. The investing amount keeps growing, but so does the lifestyle. Just slower.

25
00:02:39,302 --> 00:02:50,157
Is timeline B living like a monk? Not at all.

26
00:02:50,477 --> 00:02:53,681
B still moves out from Max's place, a couple of years later. B still gets a car, a used one. B still goes on holidays, just not five star ones.

27
00:02:54,001 --> 00:02:57,984
By twenty twenty two, B's lifestyle runs on about sixty thousand a year. Which is still fifty percent more than the noodle days.

28
00:02:58,304 --> 00:03:04,248
The difference is that twenty thousand dollars a year now flows to the future, automatically, without B ever seeing it.

29
00:03:05,048 --> 00:03:08,891
Then twenty twenty arrives, and with it the pandemic.

30
00:03:09,211 --> 00:03:12,891
For a few months, both versions of you have their hours cut at work. Same job, same cut.

31
00:03:13,211 --> 00:03:17,704
Timeline A has a car payment, a big rent and a stack of subscriptions, and no cushion. The credit card starts doing the heavy lifting.

32
00:03:18,024 --> 00:03:22,668
Timeline B has the same cut. But B's fixed costs are lower, and there's a growing pile of savings behind them. B is worried, but not scared.

33
00:03:22,988 --> 00:03:25,809
That's the hidden gift of a smaller lifestyle. It's not just cheaper. It's sturdier.

34
00:03:26,609 --> 00:03:31,253
Now let's run timeline B through real market history. Every month, half of the raises go into an S and P five hundred index fund, at real prices, starting in twenty twelve.

35
00:03:31,573 --> 00:03:38,876
The first years are small. A couple of thousand a year.

36
00:03:39,196 --> 00:03:45,767
Then the contributions grow with every raise, and the market does its thing.

37
00:03:46,087 --> 00:03:52,740
By September twenty twenty six, timeline B has put in one hundred and eighty five thousand dollars.

38
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:55,753
And it's worth about three hundred and ninety two thousand.

39
00:03:56,553 --> 00:03:58,748
And timeline A in twenty twenty six? Earning the same eighty thousand.

40
00:03:59,068 --> 00:04:08,332
A nicer apartment. A nicer car. A lot of nice dinners.

41
00:04:08,652 --> 00:04:16,570
And roughly zero dollars invested. One surprise bill away from the credit card.

42
00:04:16,890 --> 00:04:23,450
Both timelines earned exactly the same money. One of them simply kept a piece of it.

43
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:28,484
And if you asked both of them which life felt better day to day, the honest answer might surprise you. Mostly, it felt about the same.

44
00:04:28,804 --> 00:04:35,967
And timeline B didn't need a single genius stock pick to get there. Just a rule, made once, and followed every year.

45
00:04:36,767 --> 00:04:39,832
Twenty twenty three. Both timelines hear the same terrible noise from the car.

46
00:04:40,152 --> 00:04:49,359
Two thousand dollars to fix it.

47
00:04:49,679 --> 00:04:55,263
Timeline A puts it on the credit card. At average card rates of over twenty percent, that repair will cost a lot more than two thousand by the time it's paid off.

48
00:04:55,583 --> 00:04:57,592
Timeline B pays from savings, sighs, and moves on. The repair costs exactly two thousand dollars.

49
00:04:57,912 --> 00:05:01,522
Same car. Same noise. One timeline pays extra for being broke.

50
00:05:02,322 --> 00:05:05,620
But the biggest difference isn't the number. It's what the number lets you do.

51
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:14,276
When timeline B's boss starts being unbearable, B can look for a new job without panicking about next month's rent.

52
00:05:14,596 --> 00:05:21,086
When an opportunity shows up, like a course, a move, or a small business idea, B can afford to try it.

53
00:05:21,406 --> 00:05:29,266
Money in the bank doesn't just buy things. It buys options. And options feel a lot like freedom.

54
00:05:29,586 --> 00:05:33,846
Timeline A has a nicer car. Timeline B has a way out.

55
00:05:34,646 --> 00:05:37,537
Why does this happen to so many people? Because of something called hedonic adaptation.

56
00:05:37,857 --> 00:05:46,251
A new car feels amazing for a few weeks. Then it's just your car. A bigger apartment feels huge, until it's just home.

57
00:05:46,571 --> 00:05:52,492
The happiness fades, but the bill stays. Every month. And it makes the next raise feel necessary instead of extra.

58
00:05:52,812 --> 00:05:59,894
Timeline A isn't greedy or stupid. It just never decided where the raise should go, so the raise decided for itself.

59
00:06:00,694 --> 00:06:05,977
And it's not just you. Look at the whole country.

60
00:06:06,297 --> 00:06:09,339
In the US, the personal saving rate in October twenty twenty six was about four point one percent. Roughly four cents of every dollar people had after taxes.

61
00:06:09,659 --> 00:06:11,482
In twenty twenty, during lockdowns, it briefly shot above thirty percent, because there was nothing to spend money on. Then it fell right back.

62
00:06:11,802 --> 00:06:13,706
Meanwhile, average hourly pay almost doubled since two thousand six. Higher pay hasn't automatically meant more saving.

63
00:06:14,506 --> 00:06:17,664
Max, by the way, did timeline A on hard mode.

64
00:06:17,984 --> 00:06:26,018
Every raise, he upgraded. Every bonus, he upgraded. When there was no raise, he upgraded on a credit card and called it an investment in himself.

65
00:06:26,338 --> 00:06:32,770
In twenty twenty six, Max earns more than ever, and he asks you for twenty dollars until Friday.

66
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:41,042
You give it to him. You can afford it now.

67
00:06:41,362 --> 00:06:45,542
Then you tell him about the half and half rule. He laughs. Then he goes quiet. Then he asks how to set up an automatic transfer.

68
00:06:45,862 --> 00:06:50,634
Max's next raise, for the first time in his life, doesn't completely disappear.

69
00:06:51,434 --> 00:06:54,313
So how do you become timeline B? Three simple moves.

70
00:06:54,633 --> 00:07:02,922
One. Decide before the raise arrives. Write down the split, like half and half, or even a quarter for the future. Any split beats no split.

71
00:07:03,242 --> 00:07:09,268
Two. Make it automatic on the day the new salary starts. If the money never lands in your spending account, you won't miss it.

72
00:07:09,588 --> 00:07:17,808
Three. Be careful with upgrades that come with monthly bills. A nice dinner is a one time cost. A bigger lease is a cost for years.

73
00:07:18,128 --> 00:07:22,191
And enjoy the half you keep for life. That's the point. Timeline B is happy too.

74
00:07:22,991 --> 00:07:28,262
And the honest part. This is not financial advice.

75
00:07:28,582 --> 00:07:31,601
The salary here is an example, taxes are ignored to keep it simple, and the investing numbers use real past prices that don't promise the future.

76
00:07:31,921 --> 00:07:33,639
Your split depends on your life. If you have expensive debt, paying that off first is often the best upgrade of all.

77
00:07:34,439 --> 00:07:39,606
Same raises. Same job. One timeline has a nicer car. The other has a choice.

78
00:07:39,926 --> 00:07:43,002
Tell me in the comments: where did your last raise go?

79
00:07:43,322 --> 00:07:45,157
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

